Q: FastAPI returning &quot;Out of range float values are not JSON compliant&quot; with Pandas NaN values A: <p><code>df.to_dict(orient=&quot;records&quot;)</code> leaves <code>np.nan</code> in the returned Python objects; serialize with Pandas at the API boundary instead. JSON numbers do not permit <code>NaN</code>, causing the <code>Out of range float values are not JSON compliant</code> error. Pandas' <a href="https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/reference/api/pandas.DataFrame.to_json.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>DataFrame.to_json</code></a> converts <code>NaN</code>/<code>None</code> to JSON <code>null</code> via serialization rather than by replacing values in <code>df</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>import json
import pandas as pd
import numpy as np
from fastapi import FastAPI

app = FastAPI()

@app.get(&quot;/data&quot;)
def get_data():
    df = pd.DataFrame({
        &quot;id&quot;: [1, 2, 3],
        &quot;value&quot;: [10.5, np.nan, 30.2],
    })

    # do all vectorized pandas/numpy work before this line
    return json.loads(df.to_json(orient=&quot;records&quot;))
</code></pre>
<p>This maintains the return type as a list of dicts: Pandas creates valid JSON with <code>null</code>, then <code>json.loads</code> converts those back into Python <code>None</code> values for FastAPI.</p>
<p>If response speed is critical, return the JSON string directly:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>from fastapi import Response

@app.get(&quot;/data&quot;)
def get_data():
    df = pd.DataFrame({
        &quot;id&quot;: [1, 2, 3],
        &quot;value&quot;: [10.5, np.nan, 30.2],
    })

    return Response(
        content=df.to_json(orient=&quot;records&quot;),
        media_type=&quot;application/json&quot;,
    )
</code></pre>
